From: Xios project <xios@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] Add on-screen touch controls

Makes Crispy Doom playable on a touchscreen with no keyboard. Doom only:
heretic, hexen and strife link the new file but never enable it.

In a level: a floating move stick on the left half of the screen (analog,
runs past 80% of its radius), drag on the right half to turn, and FIRE,
USE and NEXT WEAPON buttons on the right. MENU sits top left, MAP top
right. On menus, the title screen and demos only MENU is shown: tap is
Enter, swipes are the arrow keys, and on a yes/no prompt tap is "y".
On the intermission and finale a tap holds use for a tic, which is what
skips them.

Everything becomes ordinary input for the existing bindings: movement is
an ev_joystick on the analog path (use_analog, on by default), run is
key_speed, turning is ev_mouse x motion scaled by the mouse sensitivity,
and the buttons press key_fire, key_use, key_nextweapon (or the next
weapon mouse button while that key is unbound), key_map_toggle and
key_menu_activate. Nothing touches ticcmds directly, so demos and
netgames see normal input.

The overlay is drawn with the SDL renderer after the frame is copied and
before it is presented, in window pixels. Held keys are released on
finger up, on an unknown or cancelled touch, on focus loss, on any
screen change and when the overlay hides. A physical key or mouse event
hides the overlay until the next touch.

SDL is told not to turn touches into mouse clicks, and pointer events
the compositor emulates from a touch (while a finger is down, or within
300 ms of one) are dropped along with their relative motion. SDL also
starts with text input on, which raises the on-screen keyboard; with the
touch controls enabled it stays off except between I_StartTextInput()
and I_StopTextInput() (savegame names, chat, numeric entry).

XIOS_TOUCH_CONTROLS=0 in the environment turns all of it off.

